{"name":"Blasphemous Blue","hex":"#3356aa","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"blasphemous","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Lacking piety or respect for the sacred; resembling blasphemy."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blasphemous"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Blasphemy","blocks":[{"text":"Blasphemy is an insult that shows contempt, disrespect or lack of reverence concerning a deity, an object considered sacred, or something that is considered inviolable. Some religions, especially Abrahamic ones, regard blasphemy as a crime, including insulting the Islamic prophet Muhammad in Islam, speaking the sacred name in Judaism, and blasphemy of God's Holy Spirit, which is an eternal sin in Christianity. It was also a crime under English common law, and it is still a crime under Italian law."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blasphemy"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/Brooklyn_Museum_-_The_Execution_of_Mansur_Hallaj_From_the_Warren_Hastings_Album.jpg/330px-Brooklyn_Museum_-_The_Execution_of_Mansur_Hallaj_From_the_Warren_Hastings_Album.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/Brooklyn_Museum_-_The_Execution_of_Mansur_Hallaj_From_the_Warren_Hastings_Album.jpg/330px-Brooklyn_Museum_-_The_Execution_of_Mansur_Hallaj_From_the_Warren_Hastings_Album.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Sufi teacher Mansur Al-Hallaj was executed in Baghdad amid political intrigue and charges of blasphemy in 922.[48]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABrooklyn_Museum_-_The_Execution_of_Mansur_Hallaj_From_the_Warren_Hastings_Album.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/31/Schnorr_von_Carolsfeld_Bibel_in_Bildern_1860_102.png/500px-Schnorr_von_Carolsfeld_Bibel_in_Bildern_1860_102.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/31/Schnorr_von_Carolsfeld_Bibel_in_Bildern_1860_102.png/500px-Schnorr_von_Carolsfeld_Bibel_in_Bildern_1860_102.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Nathan confronts David over his sex scandal with Bathsheba the wife of Uriah the Hittite, saying \"by this deed you have given occasion to the enemies of the LORD to blaspheme\" (2 Samuel 12:14).","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASchnorr_von_Carolsfeld_Bibel_in_Bildern_1860_102.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/comm